Currency quotes specify numerator and denominator; cross-rates combine compatible quotes.|₹80 per dollar and $1.25 per euro imply ₹100 per euro.|The euro cross-rate is…?|₹100|₹64|₹81.25
Spot FX settles under market conventions; forwards fix exchange for a future date.|An importer locks a future dollar payment in rupees.|A forward quote is necessarily a forecast of future spot?|No|Yes|Only for major currencies
PPP links exchange values to relative prices in theory, with practical frictions and horizons.|The same goods basket differs in currency-adjusted cost.|PPP guarantees immediate exchange-rate convergence?|No|Yes|Only for traded goods
Covered parity uses a currency hedge in equivalent funding trades; uncovered parity concerns unhedged expected exchange movements.|Forward pricing reflects rate differentials under no-arbitrage assumptions.|Uncovered parity guarantees realised unhedged returns?|No|Yes|Only with equal rates
Forward premiums and discounts compare forward with spot using an explicit quote convention.|₹82/$ forward versus ₹80/$ spot means dollars cost more rupees forward.|Quote direction must be stated?|Yes|No|Only when rates differ
Carry trades borrow lower-rate currency and invest higher-rate currency while bearing exchange and funding risk.|A 5% interest advantage is overwhelmed by a 10% adverse currency move.|Positive interest carry guarantees total profit?|No|Yes|Only annually
Transaction exposure concerns contracted flows; translation concerns reporting conversion; economic exposure concerns competitive cash effects.|A domestic firm faces foreign competitors despite no foreign invoices.|It can still have…?|Economic FX exposure|No currency-related risk|Only translation exposure
Currency hedges use forwards, options, swaps, or natural offsets matched to exposures.|Dollar export receipts offset part of dollar import payments.|Hedge gross exposure without considering offsets automatically?|No|Yes|Only for firms
Cross-border investing and financing adds currency, legal, tax, and transfer risks.|A high local return becomes negative after adverse currency conversion.|Assess only local return?|No|Yes|Only for bonds
International capital costs match currency, business exposure, country risk, and financing claims.|A dollar cash forecast uses a consistent dollar required return.|Registered country alone determines all discount rates?|No|Yes|Only for multinationals
Country and political risk includes policy changes, expropriation, conflict, and transfer restrictions.|A profitable subsidiary cannot remit cash due to restrictions.|Profit equals immediately accessible parent cash?|No|Yes|Only after audit
Multinational budgeting models local operations and parent cash, including tax, currency, remittance, and financing effects.|Local project value differs from parent value when cash is trapped.|Ignore remittance constraints?|No|Yes|Only if profitable
Trade finance supports shipment and payment; letters of credit depend on documents and terms; collections have different bank obligations.|A documentary credit pays against complying documents under its terms.|It automatically guarantees the goods' quality?|No|Yes|Only for imports
Export credit and guarantees support specified buyer or political risks, subject to coverage limits and conditions.|A guarantee covers only eligible events and a stated percentage.|All export losses are automatically covered?|No|Yes|Only for governments
Foreign-currency debt may lower quoted rates but adds exchange and refinancing exposure.|Dollar debt stays constant while the rupee depreciates.|Its rupee repayment burden generally…?|Rises|Falls|Stays unchanged
Global diversification spreads economic exposures but adds currency, legal, and correlated-shock risks.|Foreign equities diversify sectors yet remain exposed to a global recession.|International holdings eliminate systemic risk?|No|Yes|Only across many countries
